{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction grounds for criminal convictions and civil liability","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of or liable for assault or threats against the landlord, the landlord's family or employees, human trafficking, or theft of property, or who knowingly permits such a person to reside there; suit must be brought within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suits barred after 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the New Jersey Eviction Law; the grounds are convictions, juvenile delinquency findings or civil liability for the listed offenses.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"The civil-liability ground does not apply to a tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found by the court to be delinquent due to an offense involving assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, a member of the landl ord’s family or an employee of the landlord.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by court order and only for the listed causes; three months' notice to quit applies to at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month to month-to-month.","key_value":"3-month notice to quit; 1-month notice for month-to-month","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to the Act's exceptions.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"rooming and boarding houses (at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms)"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house if it does not meet one (1) of the exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act (N.J.S.A. 55:13B-3)."]},"exemptions":"Exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act include hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal for a tenant's exercise of the listed civil rights; the tenant may sue for damages.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (owner occupancy, demolition)","requirement":"A landlord who gave an owner-occupancy notice must occupy the unit at least six months; one who gave a demolition or retirement notice may not use the property residentially for five years. Violations bring treble damages plus fees and up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"6 months' occupancy; 5-year bar; treble damages; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Applies to landlords who obtained a tenant's vacancy by notice of personal occupancy, or of boarding up, demolishing or retiring the building from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"landlord gave notice of personal occupancy or of permanent demolition/retirement from residential use","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs, and a civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants who are 62 or older, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans, and who lived in a building being converted for at least a year before the conversion recording, may be protected from eviction for 40 years, subject to income limits.","key_value":"40 years' protection","coverage_conditions":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; tenants must qualify by age, disability, prior residence and income.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"}],"per_tenancy":"tenant at least 62 years old before the conversion recording, or permanently disabled, or a disabled veteran; must have lived in the building at least one year before the recording","coverage_quotes":["live in a building being converted to a condominium, cooperative; or fee simple ownership of units at least one (1) year prior to the conversion recording date."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"For qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in a qualified county, who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act, the Act extends protected-tenancy protections.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties; at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"qualified counties; at present only Hudson County qualifies"}],"per_tenancy":"tenant was not eligible for protected tenancy as a senior citizen or disabled person under the 1981 Act","coverage_quotes":["At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b))."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice requirements","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; before evicting a tenant where the buyer will personally occupy, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and the new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rental units in foreclosed property; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"foreclosed property purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as a primary residence; new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibited harassment: rent increase above rent control or unconscionable increase","requirement":"A landlord may not harass a tenant by raising rent above any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not rent-controlled, by an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
